A static field shared by every objectAt the top, the Account class holds one static field, lastNumberIssued, with the value 1003. Below it, three Account objects each hold their own number and balance: AC-1001 with 500.00, AC-1002 with 2500.00 and AC-1003 with 1500.00. Dashed lines connect each object to the single shared field in the class.Account, the classstatic lastNumberIssued 1003AC-1001AC-1002AC-1003balance  500.00balance 2500.00balance 1500.00one copy,in the classone balance per object, an instance field <p>Instance field</p> <p>Belongs to one object. Every account has its own balance.</p> <pre><code>private double balance;
ada.getBalance();
</code></pre> vs <p><code>static</code> field</p> <p>Belongs to the class. One copy, shared by every account.</p> <pre><code>private static int lastNumberIssued;
Account.getAccountsOpened();
</code></pre> <p>The differenceAsk whether every object needs its own value. If yes, it is an instance field. If it is one fact about all of them together, it is <code>static</code>. A <code>static</code> method has no <code>this</code>, so it cannot use instance fields directly.</p> Check yourself<code>lastNumberIssued</code> starts at 1000 and every constructor runs <code>lastNumberIssued++</code>. After three accounts are created, how many copies of the field exist, and what do they hold? <p>One copy, holding 1003. Each account has its own <code>balance</code>, but they all share <code>lastNumberIssued</code>.</p> <p>L3.2 What belongs to whom</p>
